# AMO Dashboard — Start Here

This project keeps cross-session context in files at the repo root, shared across tools (Claude Code, Cursor, any model) so nobody has to re-derive context from scratch each session:

- **`SESSION_LOG.md`** — dated, dense summaries of what happened in past sessions: findings, decisions, in-progress work, open threads. Read it before starting substantive work. Append a new dated entry at the end of any substantive session (facts, not a transcript — keep it dense so it stays cheap to read).
- **`CLAUDE.md`** — short, durable operational facts (deploy process, LLM provider, workflow conventions). Keep both files in sync if either changes.
- **`docs/CONFLUENCE_AMO_DASHBOARD.md`** — the Confluence page: what the tool is, how to use it, developer maintenance, and current status. This is the one document a non-developer reads.

## Closing a session — two files, always

1. **`SESSION_LOG.md`** — append a dated entry. Dense, facts only.
2. **`docs/CONFLUENCE_AMO_DASHBOARD.md`** — update whichever sections the session's work affects (description, how-to-use, architecture, developer maintenance/runbooks), **always re-check section 7 "Current status"** (production numbers, component status table, known gaps, risk register), and bump the "Last reviewed" date in the header.

Commit and push both. Claude Code additionally enforces this with a `Stop` hook (`.claude/hooks/session-doc-reminder.sh`); Cursor has no equivalent, so in Cursor it is on you to do it.

## Operational facts (also in CLAUDE.md — kept in sync)

- **Deploy:** production runs on a DigitalOcean droplet (`165.22.35.75`, `/opt/amo-dashboard`, **4 vCPU / 8GB RAM / 160GB disk** — resized 2026-07-21 from 1 vCPU/1GB for the full-history facility backfill; OCR workers auto-scale via `os.cpu_count()` capped at 4, and `run_facility_tick.sh` runs `--max-concurrent 4` sized for this box). The Node server (`dist/index.cjs`, port 5000) is **managed by PM2** under the app name `amo-dashboard` — PM2 auto-restarts it if killed, so never manually `kill` + relaunch (that fights PM2). Deploy sequence: `git pull` → (`npm install` **if `package.json` changed**) → `npm run build` → `pm2 restart amo-dashboard` (build BEFORE restart, or PM2 relaunches the old bundle). `git pull` alone does NOT update the live site. **`npm run build` deletes `dist/` before it builds** — a failed build (e.g. new dependency not installed, 7 Oct 2026) leaves production with no bundle on disk; the site survives only because PM2 holds the old one in memory, and the next restart or crash would take it down. Fix forward immediately (`npm install && npm run build`); do not restart until `dist/index.cjs` exists. Don't kill Python backfill processes when restarting. Long-running one-off scripts (e.g. `normalize.py`) must be started with `nohup ... &` + `disown` — a dropped SSH session kills plain foreground runs (add `-u` to python for unbuffered log output). **After re-running `normalize.py`, also `pm2 restart amo-dashboard`** (or `POST /api/cache/bust` from a logged-in session) — API responses are cached in-memory for 7 days (`server/cache.ts`), so the dashboard keeps serving pre-rebuild data until the cache is cleared.
- **Droplet access:** key auth is installed (`~/.ssh/id_ed25519`, passphrase held in the macOS Keychain via `ssh-add --apple-use-keychain`). **A reboot empties the ssh-agent, and then every droplet command — including the `amo-droplet` MCP tools — fails with `Permission denied (publickey)` as if the droplet were broken. Check `ssh-add -l` FIRST; `ssh-add --apple-load-keychain` restores it with no passphrase re-entry. This has cost time twice (2 and 7 Oct 2026).** Claude ran the full 2026-08-06 deploy — pull, build, `pm2 restart`, apply approvals, `normalize.py`, cache-clear restart — directly over SSH at the user's instruction. This supersedes the earlier rule that all droplet actions were manual. Still ask before acting on production: it is live, and the user decides when to deploy.
- **New server env vars need `--update-env`:** PM2 holds the environment it was started with and does not read `.env` (only the cron wrappers do). To add a variable the Node server reads (e.g. `OPENAI_API_KEY` for Ask the Data; the `MI_*` pair that FDIC Data Analytics needed from 7 to 27 Oct 2026 is gone — the bank pages read `bank_stress.db` now and need no variable): add it to `/opt/amo-dashboard/.env`, `export` **only that variable** in the shell, then `pm2 restart amo-dashboard --update-env && pm2 save`. Still export only the variables you mean to change under `--update-env`, never `source .env` wholesale: since 26 Oct 2026 `.env` holds the same `AMO_PASSWORD`/`AMO_SECRET` PM2 runs with (copied from PM2 on the owner's instruction, so a lost PM2 dump is recoverable), but any future edit to either file that is not mirrored in the other would silently change the dashboard password or sign everyone out. Keep the two in step.
- **LLM provider is OpenAI** (`gpt-5-nano` by default since 26 Oct 2026 — OpenAI retired `gpt-4.1-nano` on 23 Oct 2026 and every call 404'd for three days) for `collector/extract_pdfs.py` and `collector/enrich_entities.py` — not Claude/Anthropic. The GPT-5 family takes `max_completion_tokens` + `reasoning_effort` and (for `gpt-5-nano`) no `temperature`; `extract_pdfs.model_request_params` holds the per-family shape and `model_prices` the `--budget` prices, so change the model there, not inline. A 404 `model_not_found` now aborts a run (exit 2) instead of writing LLM_ERROR rows. The folio prompt example was removed the same day (the model had copied it into ~15,500 rows); `postprocess_extraction` drops echoed folios and `collector/clean_folio_echo.py` cleaned the stored ones. (An earlier version of this file claimed a switch to Claude/Anthropic on 2026-06-16 — that never actually shipped; ignore any references to `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in old notes.)
- **Counties:** Miami-Dade, **Broward and Hillsborough are LIVE** — Broward went end-to-end on 2026-08-10 (index → images → extraction → normalization → county-scoped UI). Its analytical coverage is still thin (539 of 42,559 documents extracted); see SESSION_LOG and ROLLBACK. Broward needs no scraping: its index comes from a public SFTP feed (`BCFTP.Broward.org:22`, `crpublic`/`crpublic`) via `collector/broward_collect.py`, which requires **`paramiko`** in the venv. Run `collector/migrate_add_county.py` before it. Document images come from the same feed via `collector/broward_images.py` — **time-critical: the feed retains only ~10 days, and missed images are unrecoverable from the free channel** (`collector/run_broward_daily.sh` is the cron wrapper). The two counties share one DB and are distinguished by a `county` column; `assignments.cfn` stays globally unique only because Miami-Dade CFNs always contain `R` and Broward instrument numbers are always pure digits — `collector/tests/check_county_isolation.py` asserts it. **Hillsborough went LIVE 2026-10-13** — free public index feed + search API, images fetched on demand at extraction time (`collector/hillsborough_collect.py`, cron `run_hillsborough_daily.sh` 13:45 UTC; not time-critical). Instrument numbers are 10 digits starting `20`; `check_county_isolation.py` holds one key pattern per county and must be given one for any new county. Hillsborough's `ASG` is a generic code, so its rows reach the analytics only once read; the weekly email holds it out until ≥98% of its 2023-onward assignments are read. Palm Beach is deferred (captcha-gated portal).
- **GitHub workflow:** every agreed-on code change should be committed and pushed to `origin/main` once confirmed.
- Local dev DB (`AMO_DB_PATH=./miami_dade_amo.db`) can lag production significantly — check actual row/date coverage before assuming it's current.
